"O7000B\"\n\nPublication year： Feb. 17, 2026  update date on preprint server： Feb. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWorld Action Models are Zero-shot Policies | Scholar Feed (https://www.scholarfeed.org/paper/2602.15922)\nciteturn31940search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last week; Adjacent 42.7 RO Feb 10VLA-JEPA: Enhancing Vision-Language-Action Model with Latent World Model ... A long-standing goal in robotics is a generalist policy that can be deployed zero-shot on new robot embodiments without per-embodiment adaptation.\n\nAdjacent 28.0 RO Feb 11\n\nLAP: Language-Action Pre-Training Enables Zero-shot Cross-Embodiment Transfer\n\nLihan Zha, Asher J. Hancock, Mingtong Zhang et al.\n\nA long-standing goal in robotics is a generalist policy that can be deployed zero-shot on new robot embodiments without per-embodiment adaptation. Despite large-scale multi-embodiment pre-training, existing Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) remain tightly coupled to their trai…\n\nAdjacent 16.9 RO Feb 23\n\nNovaPlan: Zero-Shot Long-Horizon Manipulation via Closed-Loop Video Language Planning\n\nJiahui Fu, Junyu Nan, Lingfeng Sun et al.\n\nSolving long-horizon tasks requires robots to integrate high-level semantic reasoning with low-level physical interaction. While vision-language models (VLMs) and video generation models can decompose tasks and imagine outcomes, they often lack the physical grounding necessary fo…\n\nAdjacent 31.7 RO Feb 25\n\nWorld Guidance: World Modeling in Condition Space for Action Generation\n\nYue Su, Sijin Chen, Haixin Shi et al.\n\nLeveraging future observation modeling to facilitate action generation presents a promising avenue for enhancing the capabilities of Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models. However, existing approaches struggle to strike a balance between maintaining efficient, predictable future re…\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWorld Action Models vs VLAs: Robotics Just Had Its GPT-2 Moment (https://archiesengupta.com/blog/world-action-models-vs-vla-deep-dive)\nciteturn31940search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last week; For the last two years, the default recipe for a generalist robot policy has been the VLA: take a vision-language model pretrained on the internet, bolt an action head onto it, and fine-tune on teleoperated robot demonstrations. π0, OpenVLA, GR00T N1, Helix - different flavors, same skeleton.In February 2026, NVIDIA's GEAR lab published a paper with a title that reads like a provocation: \"World Action Models are Zero-shot Policies\" (DreamZero).\n\n# World Action Models vs VLAs: Robotics Just Had Its GPT-2 Moment\n\nFor the last two years, the default recipe for a generalist robot policy has been the VLA: take a vision-language model pretrained on the internet, bolt an action head onto it, and fine-tune on teleoperated robot demonstrations. π0, OpenVLA, GR00T N1, Helix - different flavors, same skeleton.\n\nIn February 2026, NVIDIA's GEAR lab published a paper with a title that reads like a provocation: \"World Action Models are Zero-shot Policies\" (DreamZero). Jim Fan called it robotics' \"GPT-2 moment.\" A month later, a Stanford-rooted startup called Rhoda AI came out of 18 months of stealth with $450M and a claim that you can learn industrial manipulation tasks from 10-20 hours of robot data - by making a causal video model the decision-maker and demoting actions to a translation problem. NVIDIA previewed GR00T N2 at GTC as a WAM, not a VLA. 1X spun up an entire World Model Lab around the same bet.\n\nI've spent the last few weeks reading every one of these papers and tech reports side by side, and I think this is the most interesting architecture debate happening anywhere in ML right now - more interesting, honestly, than anything happening in pure LLM land this year. --------------------------------------------------------------------